WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.372
Book Three, Proposition Four.

2
00:00:02.372 --> 00:00:09.708
If in a circle two segments cut one another which are not through the center, they do not bisect one another.

3
00:00:09.708 --> 00:00:17.650
Let A B C D be a circle, and in it let two chords A C and B D, which are not through the center, cut one another at E.

4
00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:22.173
I say that A C and B D do not bisect one another.

5
00:00:22.173 --> 00:00:29.710
For, if possible, let them bisect one another, so that A E equals E C, and B E equals E D.

6
00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:35.320
Take the center F of the circle A B C D by Three One, and join F E.

7
00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:46.005
Since the segment F E through the center bisects the chord A C not through the center, by Three Three it also cuts it at right angles; therefore angle F E A is right.

8
00:00:46.005 --> 00:00:54.848
Again, since F E bisects the chord B D not through the center, by Three Three it also cuts B D at right angles; therefore angle F E B is right.

9
00:00:54.848 --> 00:01:02.949
But angle F E A was also proved right; therefore angle F E A equals angle F E B, the less equal to the greater—which is impossible.

10
00:01:02.949 --> 00:01:06.598
Therefore the chords A C and B D do not bisect one another.

11
00:01:06.598 --> 00:01:08.020
Which was to be proved.

12
00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:23.314
Proposition Three Four is an immediate, crystalline consequence of Three One and Three Three: at any non-central point E inside a circle, only one chord can be perpendicular to the radial segment F E, so at most one chord through E can be bisected there.

13
00:01:23.314 --> 00:01:27.823
Only diameters passing through the center itself mutually bisect one another.

14
00:01:27.823 --> 00:01:40.120
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G one hundred one synthesizes seven direct rules across forty ancestor nodes, applying the perpendicular-bisector equivalence of G one hundred twice at the same intersection point.

15
00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:54.455
